    SENATE COMMITTEE ON AGRICULTURE AND WATER RESOURCES
                    Jim Costa, Chairman


SB 1245 (Costa)
As amended April 10, 1997
Hearing Date: April 15, 1997
Fiscal:  Yes
Consultant:  Linda Adams

PURPOSE OF BILL:   To require a report from the Department of  
Water Resources to report on the status of groundwater  
management plans throughout the state.

BACKGROUND:

Legislation enacted in 1992 (AB 3030, Costa) established a  
process whereby local  agency water suppliers may adopt and  
implement local groundwater management plans within all or a  
portion of their service areas.  Recognizing that political  
boundaries and groundwater basins do not coincide, AB 3030  
encourages local agencies within the same groundwater basin to  
adopt and coordinate plans through joint powers agreements or  
memorandums of understanding.

PROPOSED LAW:

Requires the Department of Water Resources,  by April 1, 1998,  
to prepare and publish a report on the status of groundwater  
management plans adopted and implemented under the provisions of  
AB 3030 and to periodically update the report on an as needed  
basis.

Requires the report to include at least all of the following  
information:  a) The number of  plans in the early stages of  
development; b) The number of  plans for which resolutions of  
intention have been adopted; c) The number of  plans abandoned  
due to lack of interest or opposition; 
d) The number of  plans formally adopted; e) The number of   
plans rejected. 

COMMENTS:

AB 3030 originally contained a reporting requirement; however,  
the reporting was repealed last session in a blanket waiver of  
state agency reporting requirements.  The author of AB 3030 and  









the Department of Water Resources feel that this issue is of  
significant importance so as to warrant a report to the  
Legislature.  The Department currently has a draft report in  
preparation.

The specifics of the reporting requirement contained in SB 1245  
responds to local requests for a level of detail that will allow  
local agencies in the early stages of  AB 3030 plans to be  
included in the report and credited with their efforts. 

SUPPORT:  None received

OPPOSED:  None received



































